[b]Kitsunebi[/b], a night glass interface for 7 Days to Die V3.x: deep indigo glass panels, fox-fire cyan accents and gold titles, across every screen in the game.

The theme is XML and images only. It changes how the game looks, never how it plays, and it runs with EAC on.

[h2]About the screenshots[/h2]
The screenshots were taken with [b]separate[/b] inventory and container mods installed (a bigger backpack, extra crafting queue and workstation slots, bigger stacks), to show the theme stretching to fit them. Those mods are [b]not included[/b] in KitsuneUI. With KitsuneUI alone, your bags and workstations keep their vanilla sizes.

[h2]What it covers[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]HUD:[/b] health, stamina, food and water bars with an injury hatch, buff pop-outs lined up with the bars, compass, location box, quest and recipe trackers, party bars, enemy health bar
[*][b]Toolbelt:[/b] a glass tray that grows to fit one or two bars
[*][b]Inventory:[/b] backpack, loot, vehicle and drone storage, with rounded slots that glow fox-fire when selected and gold on hover
[*][b]Crafting:[/b] recipe list, recipe and item details, crafting queue, the creative menu, every workstation
[*][b]Trader and quests:[/b] trader stock, services, job offer, quest journal, turn-in rewards
[*][b]Character:[/b] character sheet, buffs, skills and perks, challenges, cosmetics
[*][b]Map:[/b] map, waypoints, shared waypoints and the right-click menus
[*][b]Menus:[/b] main menu, Play Game, the in-game Esc menu, every Options page, death message and respawn screen
[*][b]Loading screen:[/b] the picture fills the screen and the tip sits in a glass column down the right edge, with the spawn buttons at its foot
[*][b]Everything else:[/b] chat, the player list, power and trap windows, cameras, signs and the sign editor, keypads, tooltips, context menus, tips, message boxes, admin and debug panels
[/list]

[h2]Optional add-ons[/h2]
Four small extras, each in its own zip. Toolbelt20, JobPreview and Settings contain a DLL, so they need [b]EAC off[/b]; Loading is XML and images only.
[list]
[*][b]KitsuneUI_Toolbelt20:[/b] two toolbelt bars of 10. Number keys use the bottom bar, shift + number uses the top bar (marked with a SHIFT tag, and its slot numbers are gold). Turns on the toolbelt size setting the game already has but only reads in the prefab editor. Works with or without KitsuneUI.
[*][b]KitsuneUI_JobPreview:[/b] trader jobs as picture tiles under the trader, with each location's preview image, name, tier and distance, and a job offer window with the location's picture, distance, biome and objectives. With the Settings add-on it can be switched back to the game's dialog list. Needs KitsuneUI.
[*][b]KitsuneUI_Settings:[/b] an in-game settings window (Esc menu > KitsuneUI). See below. Needs KitsuneUI.
[*][b]KitsuneUI_Loading:[/b] six Kitsunebi night paintings for the loading screen in place of the vanilla pictures, plus four KitsuneUI tips. With the Settings add-on the pictures take turns, so you see every one. On a server, install it on the server and every player's game.
[/list]

[h2]The Settings add-on[/h2]
Esc menu > KitsuneUI opens a settings window in five tabs. The defaults match KitsuneUI without the add-on, so nothing changes until you flip a switch. It's saved per player, so on a server everyone picks their own.
[list]
[*][b]HUD:[/b] toolbelt slot numbers, enemy health bar always shown, [b]fade the toolbelt and stat bars when idle[/b], a [b]horde night countdown[/b] under the day and time, [b]crafting progress[/b] on the HUD, an [b]action bar[/b] while you reload, eat, drink or use medicine, [b]time left beside buffs[/b], and a [b]warning ring[/b] on toolbelt items that are nearly worn out, and with two toolbelt bars a [b]one-row toolbelt[/b] (all 20 slots side by side)
[*][b]Map:[/b] a [b]minimap[/b] drawn from your explored map, with the map's markers (quest targets, waypoints, bed, backpack, vehicles, party), zoom (64, 128 or 256 blocks), north up or turn with me (following the vehicle you drive too), names for players and waypoints, a [b]square or round[/b] map, your coordinates and the day and time under it, and [b]creatures[/b] as dots (animals, or animals and zombies)
[*][b]Combat:[/b] [b]ElvUI-style unit frames[/b] by the crosshair (yours and your target's, with buff and debuff icons above them), [b]party frames[/b] under yours, [b]nameplates[/b] over enemies you're fighting, [b]floating combat text[/b], frame width, buff icon size and how many buffs to show, [b]debuffs in their own row[/b] with a countdown sweep on every icon, and [b]portraits[/b] (a real picture of every zombie, trader and bandit, painted ones for animals, and a snapshot of your own character)
[*][b]Windows:[/b] [b]show the whole backpack[/b] without scrolling, a [b]search box[/b] in the backpack that dims everything that doesn't match, trader jobs as picture tiles (at 100 to 150%), [b]backpack width[/b] (Normal, Wide, Extra wide) with a whole-backpack button in the bag, text size (90 to 130%), [b]quality rings[/b] on item slots, [b]compare with the item in your hand[/b], and [b]quest tracker progress bars[/b] with a direction arrow, and [b]hide job tiers[/b] (trader jobs nearest first, no difficulty skulls: scout the danger yourself)
[*][b]Look:[/b] glass strength (100/85/70/55%), a [b]glass colour[/b] (Midnight, Charcoal, Obsidian, Forest, Plum, Ember, Ocean), colour presets (Kitsunebi, Ember, Moss, Sakura) or a [b]custom colour[/b] from 12 hues, bar text (100/100, 100%, value only or none), a [b]separate XP bar[/b] above the toolbelt, and [b]15 bar styles[/b] for every bar on the HUD (Flat, Smooth, Glossy, Neon, Brushed and more)
[/list]
[b]Move HUD[/b] lets you drag, resize or hide every HUD piece on a grid with optional snap, and [b]split stat bars[/b] makes them two pieces, Health & Stamina and Food & Water. [b]Profiles[/b] save your whole layout by name, and you can export one as a line of text to share with friends. [b]Presets[/b] (Minimal, Full, Streamer) switch HUD pieces on and off in one click.

[h2]Install[/h2]
Unzip each zip you want into your [b]Mods[/b] folder, so you end up with Mods/KitsuneUI/ModInfo.xml. The Mods folder is in your game folder, or at %APPDATA%\7DaysToDie\Mods.

[b]Playing on a server:[/b] install it on the [b]server and on every player's game[/b]. The server sends its UI layout to everyone who joins, so a player without KitsuneUI sees blank spots where the theme's images go. Tell your players before you add it.

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]
Windows that other mods resize are restyled in place and keep their vanilla names. Their glass panels are anchored to the grids rather than sized with fixed numbers, so bigger bags, containers, toolbelts and workstation grids still fit.

Mods that rebuild the same windows from scratch (full UI overhauls) will clash with it. Use one UI theme at a time.

KitsuneUI's own text (settings window, move mode, SHIFT and TIP tags) is translated into all 13 languages the game supports and follows your game's language setting.

Built and tested on 7 Days to Die V3.3.

[b]For mod authors:[/b] your mod's windows can wear the Kitsunebi glass, follow each player's look settings and join Move HUD, and stay vanilla for players without KitsuneUI. See [url=https://github.com/Kitsune-Den/KitsuneUI/blob/main/docs/framework.md]Building on KitsuneUI[/url].

[h2]Removing it[/h2]
Delete the folders. The theme stores nothing in your save. Removing the Toolbelt20 add-on is safe for items too: the extra toolbelt items go into your backpack, and anything that doesn't fit drops at your feet.

[h2]Credits[/h2]
Inspired by Terminal UI by SkyLett. All art and code here is our own.
